s the stripped copy, it mounts the full host repository read-only at `/repo`: holdout fixtures, the holdout seed, git history and env files. The repository's own test catches this and fails.\n2. **Batch 2 was readable during the build.** Per `timings.jsonl`, the builder pass ran from about 12:40 to 13:11. The batch 2 holdout fixtures were being recorded into the host repository from 12:40:13 to 15:08, so they were visible through `/repo`. `measurements.md` says the builder \"never saw batch 2\".\n3. **The clone can rewrite the scorer.** The clone runs inside the verifier's own process, and the lock doesn't cover it. In a scratch copy, a clone that answers 404 to every request, plus a one-line patch to a built-in sort function, scored 696 of 696.\n4. **The determinism check compares nothing** (`verify.ts:251`). It counts any two outputs as identical, so the report always shows 100 %.\n5. **Noise recording is broken** (`record.ts:38`). It compares the first recording against an empty object and ignores the second. Any newly recorded fixture would have every field marked as noise and pass against any clone. The 1818 committed fixtures all have empty noise lists, so they weren't made by this code.\n6. **The committed scores came from a different scorer.** They were produced under lock `0cf3b94b0704`, but the committed lock is `957ef3a28069`. The history is a single \"Snapshot\" commit, so the change can't be reviewed.\n\n**Other isolation and integrity defects**\n- **Holdout IDs leak again.** `report.md`, which the builder sandbox receives, lists every holdout case by ID. My full run produced 1122 such lines, reintroducing a leak `docs/lessons.md` says was fixed.\n- **Environment-variable bypass.** Setting `RH_SANDBOX=1` while git is unavailable skips the committed-lock check, so a locally relocked, tampered scorer can be scored.\n- **The guard hook doesn't hold.**\n  - The top-level session in the container runs as \"main\", which the guard exempts entirely.\n  - For s